<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kaitag Utsmiyate</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1820 CE (approx.) · capital Majalis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Dargin and Kumyk principality of Dagestan ruled by the utsmi, known today for its distinctive Kaitag embroideries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kaitag, in the foothills and mountains west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, was ruled by a dynasty bearing the title utsmi from at least the 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> had become dominant there by then, after a long history of mixed beliefs; Arab geographers had earlier described the region as partly Christian and pagan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The utsmi ruled through nobles over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dargins">Dargin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumyks">Kumyk</a> villages from Majalis and other seats, with free communities keeping their own customs. The economy combined farming, herding and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a>. Kaitag women produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> embroideries with bold abstract designs. Utsmi Amir Hamza captured the German naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-gottlieb-gmelin">Samuel Gmelin</a>, who died in captivity in 1774.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The utsmiyate was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> for long periods and later allied with or resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1820 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> deposed the last utsmi and annexed Kaitag.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1400 CE – 1820 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.56%;width:8.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1820 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Majalis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amir Hamza</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dargin; Kumyk</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kaitag, Utsmiyate of Kaitag, Kaytag</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1610&amp;focus=kaitag-utsmiyate" aria-label="Kaitag Utsmiyate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5642 205 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kaitag Utsmiyate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6281 1235h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5962 461h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5962 461h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1610&amp;focus=kaitag-utsmiyate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

